{"name":"Rumba Orange","hex":"#c77b42","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rumba","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A slow-paced Cuban partner dance in 4:4 time."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To dance the rumba."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rumba"},{"term":"orange","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evergreen tree of the genus Citrus such as Citrus sinensis which yields oranges (the fruit).","Any round citrus fruit with a yellow-red colour when ripe and a sour-sweet taste; the fruit of the orange tree.\n Specifically, a sweet orange or Citrus sinensis."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having the colour of the fruit of an orange tree; yellowred; reddish-yellow."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/orange"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rumba","blocks":[{"text":"The term rumba may refer to a variety of unrelated music styles. Originally, \"rumba\" was used as a synonym for \"party\" in northern Cuba, and by the late 19th century it was used to denote the complex of secular music styles known as Cuban rumba. Since the early 20th century the term has been used in different countries to refer to distinct styles of music and dance, most of which are only tangentially related to the original Cuban rumba, if at all. The vague etymological origin of the term rumba, as well as its interchangeable use with guaracha in settings such as bufo theatre, is largely responsible for such worldwide polysemy of the term. In addition, \"rumba\" was the primary marketing term for Cuban music in North America, as well as West and Central Africa, during much of the 20th century, before the rise of mambo, pachanga and salsa."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Bailarines_de_rumba_cubana_en_la_plaza_de_los_trabajadores_de_Camag%C3%BCey%2C_Cuba.jpg/330px-Bailarines_de_rumba_cubana_en_la_plaza_de_los_trabajadores_de_Camag%C3%BCey%2C_Cuba.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rumba","wikidata":"Q21684437"},{"title":"Orange (colour)","blocks":[{"text":"Orange is the colour between yellow and red on the spectrum of visible light. The human eyes perceive orange when observing light with a dominant wavelength between roughly 585 and 620 nanometres. In traditional colour theory, it is a secondary colour of pigments, produced by mixing yellow and red. In the RGB colour model, it is a tertiary colour. It is named after the fruit of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Delicatearch.png/330px-Delicatearch.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orange_(colour)","wikidata":"Q39338"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg/500px-ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg/1280px-ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg/500px-ISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AISS_Expedition_5_crew.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/%28A%29_Sadhu_India.jpg/500px-%28A%29_Sadhu_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/%28A%29_Sadhu_India.jpg/1280px-%28A%29_Sadhu_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/%28A%29_Sadhu_India.jpg/500px-%28A%29_Sadhu_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&u